Apple's new HomeView includes temperature and humidity sensing, allowing the smart home hub to monitor indoor conditions and supply readings to the new thermostats developed with LG Electronics and Schneider Electric.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/homeview-base.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1070339" /><br/>
Apple's <a href="https://www.apple.com/apple-homeview/specs/">technical specifications</a> confirm that both the tabletop and wall-mounted HomeView have a temperature and humidity sensor. The hardware is listed alongside the ambient light sensor and accelerometer.<br/>
<br/>
There are several limitations for the HomeView's readings. Temperature and humidity sensing is optimized for indoor domestic environments with ambient temperatures around 59°F to 86°F, and relative humidity around 30% to 70%.<br/>
<br/>
Accuracy may decrease when HomeView plays audio at high volume for an extended period. The sensors also require time to calibrate immediately after startup before results appear.<br/>
<br/>
According to Apple, the LG and Schneider Electric thermostats in the "<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/13/apple-home-collection/">Apple Home Collection</a>" can obtain temperature and humidity readings from multiple rooms using sensors built into HomeView, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/homepod/">HomePod</a>, and &zwnj;HomePod&zwnj; mini, as well as dedicated sensors from either manufacturer. The thermostats also support Adaptive Temperature in the Home app, which uses on-device intelligence from iPhone to adjust settings based on whether someone is home, winding down for the night, or away.<br/>
<br/>
